  ($qb$S13C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Salary benchmarking against the market in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Systematic and annual — compensation is benchmarked against relevant market data and adjusted proactively$qb$, $qb$Periodic — market benchmarking is conducted every 2-3 years$qb$, $qb$Ad hoc — benchmarking happens only when a specific retention problem forces it$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — compensation is set without reference to market data$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall pay positioning relative to the relevant talent market is:$qb$, $qb$At or above the 75th percentile — the organisation competes strongly on pay$qb$, $qb$At the 50th-75th percentile — pay is competitive$qb$, $qb$At the 25th-50th percentile — pay is below market and is a retention risk$qb$, $qb$Below the 25th percentile — pay is significantly below market and is a major talent problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The competitiveness of the total rewards package (salary, bonus, benefits) as a talent attraction and retention tool is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — total rewards are a genuine competitive advantage in the talent market$qb$, $qb$Adequate — the package is competitive though not exceptional$qb$, $qb$Below average — total rewards are a recruitment and retention disadvantage$qb$, $qb$Significantly below average — poor total rewards are a primary driver of attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Pay ranges (minimum, midpoint, maximum for each role grade) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Defined, documented, and consistently applied across the organisation$qb$, $qb$Defined but not always consistently applied$qb$, $qb$Informally understood but not formally documented$qb$, $qb$Not defined — pay is set individually without reference to pay ranges$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The salary of new joiners relative to existing employees at the same grade in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Actively managed to prevent new joiner premium (paying new hires more than existing employees)$qb$, $qb$Generally managed but occasional new joiner premium occurs$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — new joiner premium exists in some teams and is a known irritant$qb$, $qb$Unmanaged — new joiners are frequently paid more than equivalently experienced existing employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Annual salary review process in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured, transparent, and consistently applied — criteria are clear and outcomes are communicated well$qb$, $qb$Generally structured — most employees understand how increases are determined$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — salary review processes vary by department and manager$qb$, $qb$Opaque — employees do not understand how salary decisions are made$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Salary increase decisions in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Differentiated by performance — high performers receive meaningfully higher increases$qb$, $qb$Broadly differentiated — some performance-based differentiation exists$qb$, $qb$Largely uniform — most employees receive similar increases regardless of performance$qb$, $qb$Not differentiated — salary increases are applied uniformly or by cost of living only$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Cost of living adjustments in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Applied consistently and communicated clearly to all employees$qb$, $qb$Applied for most employees but not always transparently communicated$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently applied — some employees receive adjustments and others do not$qb$, $qb$Not applied — the organisation does not make cost of living adjustments$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The speed at which the organisation adjusts pay when the market moves significantly is:$qb$, $qb$Fast — pay adjustments are made within the same year as significant market moves$qb$, $qb$Within 1-2 years — adjustments follow market moves with a short lag$qb$, $qb$Within 3+ years — pay adjustments significantly lag the market$qb$, $qb$Not adjusted — the organisation does not proactively adjust pay to keep pace with the market$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, pay competitiveness in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine talent attraction and retention advantage$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — pay is competitive enough to attract and retain most of the talent needed$qb$, $qb$Is a limitation — below-market pay is creating consistent talent friction$qb$, $qb$Is a significant problem — uncompetitive pay is a primary driver of talent loss$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Gender pay analysis (whether men and women are paid equally for equivalent roles) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Conducted regularly, reported transparently, and any gaps are actively closed$qb$, $qb$Conducted periodically when required by law or reporting obligation$qb$, $qb$Conducted but findings are not acted on meaningfully$qb$, $qb$Not conducted — gender pay equity is not analysed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The gender pay gap in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Non-existent or negligible — men and women are paid equitably for equivalent roles and experience$qb$, $qb$Small — minor gaps exist and are being actively closed$qb$, $qb$Significant — a meaningful gender pay gap exists and is acknowledged but not fully addressed$qb$, $qb$Large and unaddressed — a significant gender pay gap exists without credible plans to close it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Pay equity analysis beyond gender (by ethnicity, disability, age, tenure, etc.) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive — the organisation analyses and addresses pay equity across multiple dimensions$qb$, $qb$Partially conducted — some dimensions beyond gender are analysed$qb$, $qb$Limited — analysis is restricted to gender pay without broader equity review$qb$, $qb$Not conducted — pay equity beyond gender is not analysed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Transparency of pay in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees understand pay structures, bands, and the criteria for pay decisions$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some transparency exists but employees often do not know where they sit in a pay band$qb$, $qb$Limited — pay is not discussed openly and employees have little visibility of pay structures$qb$, $qb$Very limited — pay is treated as entirely confidential with no transparency of structures or criteria$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C02Q05$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Pay decisions in your organisation are free from bias:$qb$, $qb$Yes — structured processes and regular audits ensure pay decisions are made without discriminatory bias$qb$, $qb$Largely — processes are designed to be fair though some manager discretion creates risk$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — some bias in pay decisions is known or suspected$qb$, $qb$No — pay decisions are significantly influenced by bias and this is known or suspected$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C02Q06$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's response when pay inequities are identified is:$qb$, $qb$Fast and comprehensive — inequities are immediately investigated and pay is corrected$qb$, $qb$Slow but genuine — pay inequities are eventually corrected$qb$, $qb$Minimal — inequities are acknowledged but corrections are partial or delayed$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — identified pay inequities are not corrected$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C02Q07$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Contractual terms (not just pay) are applied equitably across genders, ethnicities, and other groups:$qb$, $qb$Yes — terms and conditions are equal across all groups$qb$, $qb$Largely — most terms are equitable with minor differences being addressed$qb$, $qb$Partially — some contractual inequities exist and are not fully resolved$qb$, $qb$No — significant contractual inequities exist between groups$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C02Q08$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Employee perception of pay fairness in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees feel they are fairly paid relative to their peers and contribution$qb$, $qb$Moderate — pay fairness is broadly accepted though some concerns exist$qb$, $qb$Low — a significant proportion of employees feel unfairly paid$qb$, $qb$Very low — perceptions of pay unfairness are widespread and are damaging morale and retention$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C02Q09$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Reporting obligations around pay equity (mandatory gender pay gap reporting, etc.) are met:$qb$, $qb$Fully and proactively — the organisation meets all obligations and goes beyond what is required$qb$, $qb$Fully — all legal reporting obligations are met$qb$, $qb$Partially — most obligations are met but some gaps exist$qb$, $qb$Not met — the organisation has outstanding pay reporting obligations$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C02Q10$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, pay equity and fairness in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine organisational strength — employees trust the organisation to pay them fairly$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — pay is broadly fair with some areas for improvement$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — pay inequities are creating trust and retention problems$qb$, $qb$Is a serious issue — pay unfairness is damaging the organisation's culture, reputation, and talent$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C03Q01$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The design of variable pay (bonus, commission, incentive schemes) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strategically aligned — variable pay drives the behaviours and outcomes the organisation needs$qb$, $qb$Generally aligned — variable pay broadly rewards the right behaviours$qb$, $qb$Partially aligned — some aspects of variable pay drive the right behaviour; others create perverse incentives$qb$, $qb$Misaligned — variable pay drives behaviour that is not in the organisation's interest$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C03Q02$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The achievability of bonus and incentive targets in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Calibrated — targets are stretching but achievable and most performers receive something$qb$, $qb$Generally achievable — most performers reach at least threshold$qb$, $qb$Difficult — targets are regularly set too high and most employees receive nothing$qb$, $qb$Very difficult — incentive targets are so high that the scheme does not motivate$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C03Q03$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The transparency of variable pay calculations in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees understand exactly how their variable pay is calculated$qb$, $qb$Moderate — employees broadly understand the scheme but not always the precise calculations$qb$, $qb$Limited — employees are unclear on how their variable pay is determined$qb$, $qb$Very limited — variable pay is calculated and paid without employees understanding the methodology$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C03Q04$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The consistency of variable pay application across the organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — the same rules are applied to all eligible employees consistently$qb$, $qb$Generally consistent — minor variations exist$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — variable pay is applied differently across departments or managers$qb$, $qb$Very inconsistent — variable pay practices are highly variable and create perceived unfairness$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C03Q05$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The timing of variable pay payouts in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Reliable — payouts are made at the promised time without fail$qb$, $qb$Generally reliable — minor delays occasionally occur$qb$, $qb$Unreliable — delays in variable pay payouts are a recurring and demoralising problem$qb$, $qb$Very unreliable — employees cannot rely on receiving variable pay at the promised time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C03Q06$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Long-term incentive plans (LTIPs, ESOPs, phantom shares) in your organisation for senior roles are:$qb$, $qb$Well-designed and competitive — long-term incentives retain key people and align them to organisational success$qb$, $qb$Present — some long-term incentive plans exist$qb$, $qb$Limited — long-term incentives are available only to the most senior levels$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not use long-term incentive plans$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C03Q07$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The balance between fixed and variable pay in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-calibrated — the mix reflects the risk profile of each role and motivates appropriately$qb$, $qb$Generally appropriate — the balance works for most roles$qb$, $qb$Suboptimal — too much or too little variable pay for some role categories$qb$, $qb$Poorly calibrated — the fixed-variable mix creates motivation or retention problems$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C03Q08$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Non-monetary incentives and recognition programmes in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive and valued — non-monetary incentives meaningfully supplement financial rewards$qb$, $qb$Present — some non-monetary recognition exists$qb$, $qb$Limited — non-monetary incentives are token rather than genuinely valued$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation relies entirely on financial rewards without non-monetary recognition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C03Q09$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The perception of variable pay fairness among employees is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees believe the incentive system is fair and motivating$qb$, $qb$Moderate — broadly fair though some concerns exist$qb$, $qb$Low — a significant proportion of employees feel the incentive system is unfair$qb$, $qb$Very low — perceived incentive unfairness is a significant morale and retention problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C03Q10$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, the variable pay and incentive structures in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Are a genuine performance and retention lever — well-designed incentives drive results$qb$, $qb$Are adequate — incentives broadly work without being exceptional$qb$, $qb$Have significant gaps — incentive design problems are limiting motivation and performance$qb$, $qb$Are counterproductive — poorly designed incentives are driving the wrong behaviour or damaging morale$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C04Q01$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The comprehensiveness of employee benefits in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the benefits package covers health, retirement, leave, wellbeing, and development comprehensively$qb$, $qb$Good — the core benefits are in place and competitive$qb$, $qb$Adequate — basic benefits are provided but the package is not differentiated$qb$, $qb$Limited — the benefits package is minimal and is a talent disadvantage$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C04Q02$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Health and medical benefits in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive and competitive — medical cover is a genuine talent advantage$qb$, $qb$Adequate — standard health cover is provided$qb$, $qb$Basic — health benefits are minimal$qb$, $qb$Not provided — the organisation does not provide health or medical benefits$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C04Q03$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Retirement and pension provision in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employer contributions and investment choices are competitive$qb$, $qb$Adequate — a pension scheme exists with reasonable employer contribution$qb$, $qb$Basic — a pension scheme exists but employer contribution is minimal$qb$, $qb$Not provided — the organisation does not provide retirement savings support$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C04Q04$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Leave entitlements (annual leave, maternity, paternity, sick leave) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Above legal minimum and competitive — leave policies are a genuine talent advantage$qb$, $qb$At legal minimum and broadly competitive$qb$, $qb$At legal minimum but below market norms$qb$, $qb$Below legal minimum or compliance with leave entitlements is inconsistent$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C04Q05$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Flexibility and choice in the benefits package (allowing employees to choose benefits that suit them) is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees can flex their benefits within a defined total budget$qb$, $qb$Partial — some choice of benefits is available$qb$, $qb$Limited — the benefits package is largely fixed without meaningful choice$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — all employees receive identical benefits without any element of choice$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C04Q06$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Wellbeing benefits (mental health support, EAP, gym access, preventive health) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive — wellbeing benefits are genuinely used and valued by employees$qb$, $qb$Present — some wellbeing benefits exist$qb$, $qb$Token — wellbeing benefits exist on paper but are not used or valued$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation provides no specific wellbeing benefits$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C04Q07$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Benefits communication in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — employees are fully informed about all benefits and how to use them$qb$, $qb$Generally good — employees know about their main benefits$qb$, $qb$Limited — many employees are unaware of some benefits they are entitled to$qb$, $qb$Poor — employees do not know what benefits they have or how to access them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C04Q08$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's benefits offering relative to peer employers is:$qb$, $qb$Superior — the benefits package is a differentiated talent advantage$qb$, $qb$Competitive — benefits are broadly comparable to peer employers$qb$, $qb$Below average — benefits are below peer employer norms$qb$, $qb$Significantly below average — the benefits package is a talent disadvantage$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C04Q09$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Employee utilisation of available benefits in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees use most of the benefits available to them$qb$, $qb$Moderate — core benefits are well-used; others are underutilised$qb$, $qb$Low — many benefits are underutilised and employees are not getting full value$qb$, $qb$Very low — most benefits go unused, indicating poor communication or poor design$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C04Q10$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, the benefits and total rewards package in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage in attracting and retaining talent$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — the package is competitive enough to support recruitment and retention$qb$, $qb$Is below average — the benefits package is a persistent talent disadvantage$qb$, $qb$Is a significant talent liability — poor benefits are actively driving attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C05Q01$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Payroll accuracy in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — payroll errors are extremely rare and are corrected immediately when they occur$qb$, $qb$Good — payroll is accurate with occasional minor errors that are quickly resolved$qb$, $qb$Variable — payroll errors occur with sufficient frequency to be a known employee concern$qb$, $qb$Poor — payroll inaccuracy is a persistent problem that is damaging employee trust$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C05Q02$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Payroll processing timeliness in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Reliable — payroll is always processed and paid on the committed date$qb$, $qb$Generally reliable — minor delays occasionally occur$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — payroll timing is not always reliable and occasional delays cause concern$qb$, $qb$Unreliable — late payroll is a recurring problem with serious morale consequences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C05Q03$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Tax and statutory deduction compliance in payroll is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — all statutory deductions are calculated correctly and remitted on time$qb$, $qb$Good — compliance is maintained with rare and quickly corrected exceptions$qb$, $qb$Adequate — broadly compliant with occasional errors that are corrected$qb$, $qb$Poor — payroll compliance has resulted in penalties or employee complaints$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C05Q04$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Payslip clarity and transparency in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — payslips are clear, detailed, and employees understand every element$qb$, $qb$Adequate — payslips are broadly clear$qb$, $qb$Limited — payslips are difficult for employees to understand$qb$, $qb$Very limited — payslips are unclear and employees frequently question their contents$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C05Q05$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Management of payroll for variable or irregular pay components (overtime, commissions, expense reimbursements) is:$qb$, $qb$Accurate and timely — variable pay components are always processed correctly and promptly$qb$, $qb$Generally accurate — occasional errors are corrected quickly$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — variable pay components are sometimes processed incorrectly or delayed$qb$, $qb$Poor — inaccurate processing of variable pay components is a persistent employee frustration$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C05Q06$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Payroll audit and controls in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Robust — payroll is regularly audited with strong controls preventing errors and fraud$qb$, $qb$Adequate — basic controls are in place and periodic audits are conducted$qb$, $qb$Partial — some controls exist but the audit regime is not comprehensive$qb$, $qb$Weak — insufficient payroll controls and auditing create risk of error and fraud$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C05Q07$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Employee queries about pay are resolved in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Quickly and accurately — most pay queries are resolved within 24 hours$qb$, $qb$Generally promptly — queries are resolved within a working week$qb$, $qb$Slowly — pay queries often take more than a week to resolve$qb$, $qb$Very slowly — unresolved pay queries are a persistent employee complaint$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
