  ($qb$S13C05Q09$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Compliance with payroll-related employment law (minimum wage, equal pay, overtime) is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the organisation proactively ensures payroll compliance beyond legal minimums$qb$, $qb$Good — all key compliance requirements are met$qb$, $qb$Adequate — broadly compliant with some areas to strengthen$qb$, $qb$Poor — payroll compliance has been questioned or challenged$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C05Q10$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Overall, payroll accuracy and compliance in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine employee trust builder — payroll is reliable and employees never worry about it$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — payroll is generally accurate and compliant$qb$, $qb$Has notable issues — payroll errors and delays are a recurring source of employee frustration$qb$, $qb$Is a serious problem — payroll inaccuracy and compliance gaps are damaging trust and creating risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C06Q01$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation understand how their pay is determined:$qb$, $qb$Fully — the criteria, process, and pay ranges are communicated clearly and employees feel informed$qb$, $qb$Broadly — most employees understand the main factors that determine their pay$qb$, $qb$Partially — employees know their pay but not how it was determined$qb$, $qb$Not at all — pay determination is opaque and employees feel kept in the dark$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C06Q02$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Pay band and grade structures in your organisation are communicated to employees:$qb$, $qb$Fully — employees know their grade, pay band, and how to progress through it$qb$, $qb$Partially — employees know their grade but not the full pay band$qb$, $qb$Minimally — some information is shared but not enough for employees to benchmark themselves$qb$, $qb$Not at all — pay structures are not communicated to employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C06Q03$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation are equipped to have pay conversations with their team members:$qb$, $qb$Well — managers are trained and supported to have clear, honest pay conversations$qb$, $qb$Adequately — most managers can handle basic pay conversations$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — some managers are comfortable with pay conversations; others avoid them$qb$, $qb$Poorly — managers are not equipped to discuss pay and this creates frustration and confusion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C06Q04$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's communication around annual pay reviews is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — employees receive clear, timely communication about their outcome and the rationale$qb$, $qb$Generally good — most employees receive adequate communication$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — quality of pay review communication varies significantly by manager and department$qb$, $qb$Poor — many employees receive their pay outcome without clear explanation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C06Q05$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The organisation's willingness to discuss pay benchmarking data with employees is:$qb$, $qb$Open — market data is shared with employees to support transparency$qb$, $qb$Selective — market data is shared with managers but not broadly with employees$qb$, $qb$Limited — market data is available to senior HR only$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — pay benchmarking data is not shared with anyone below leadership$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C06Q06$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Communication about total reward value (the full monetary value of salary plus benefits) to employees is:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive — employees regularly receive a total reward statement showing full package value$qb$, $qb$Periodic — total reward statements are provided during review season$qb$, $qb$Rare — total reward value is not regularly communicated$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not communicate total reward value to employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C06Q07$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The culture of pay transparency in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is open — pay is discussed openly as a professional topic and transparency reduces speculation$qb$, $qb$Is semi-open — some transparency exists but pay remains partially taboo$qb$, $qb$Is closed — pay is treated as highly confidential and discussion is discouraged$qb$, $qb$Is very closed — discussing pay is actively discouraged and creates discomfort or consequences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C06Q08$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's response to individual pay concerns raised by employees is:$qb$, $qb$Prompt, empathetic, and transparent — concerns are addressed with honesty and relevant data$qb$, $qb$Generally prompt — most concerns are addressed$qb$, $qb$Slow — pay concerns are often not resolved in a timely way$qb$, $qb$Poor — pay concerns are dismissed or handled in ways that increase rather than reduce dissatisfaction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
