  ($qb$S13C06Q10$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, pay communication and transparency in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Builds employee trust and confidence in the organisation's compensation approach$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — employees are sufficiently informed$qb$, $qb$Has gaps — lack of pay transparency is creating speculation and dissatisfaction$qb$, $qb$Is a significant problem — poor pay communication is actively damaging employee trust and engagement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C07Q01$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation has a documented reward philosophy and strategy:$qb$, $qb$Yes — a clear reward philosophy guides all compensation decisions and is communicated$qb$, $qb$Partially — a general philosophy is understood at leadership level$qb$, $qb$Informally — some principles are shared but a formal strategy is not documented$qb$, $qb$No — compensation decisions are made without a guiding reward philosophy or strategy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C07Q02$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The reward strategy is aligned to the business strategy:$qb$, $qb$Tightly — compensation directly incentivises the behaviours and outcomes the strategy requires$qb$, $qb$Generally — reward broadly supports the business strategy$qb$, $qb$Loosely — some misalignment exists between what is rewarded and what the strategy requires$qb$, $qb$Poorly — what is rewarded is disconnected from or in tension with the business strategy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C07Q03$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's reward investment (what is spent on total compensation as a % of revenue) is:$qb$, $qb$Well-calibrated — reward investment reflects the value of talent to the business$qb$, $qb$Generally appropriate — investment is reasonable for the business model$qb$, $qb$Suboptimal — either over- or under-investment in reward is creating problems$qb$, $qb$Poorly calibrated — reward investment is significantly misaligned with business value and needs$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C07Q04$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Job evaluation and grade architecture in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Rigorous — jobs are evaluated using a consistent methodology and grades reflect genuine job weight$qb$, $qb$Generally sound — job evaluation is broadly consistent$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — job grades are applied inconsistently leading to perceived fairness issues$qb$, $qb$Not in place — jobs are graded without a consistent evaluation methodology$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S13C07Q05$qb$, $qb$S13$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Pay differentiation between high and low performers in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Significant and meaningful — high performers are rewarded significantly more than average performers$qb$, $qb$Present — some differentiation exists but it is not as pronounced as it should be$qb$, $qb$Minimal — pay differentiation between high and average performers is insufficient to motivate$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — pay is applied uniformly regardless of performance level$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
